    "done_when": "At the committed head: (1) the picker's competence and context verdicts for all candidates of one pick are computed from a single shared ledger read rather than one read per candidate, by passing preloaded inputs into the existing verdicts in reckon/crew/routing.py without changing their results; a test in tests/test_picker_latency.py, a new file, asserts the ledger loader is called exactly once per pick for a nine-candidate fixture, and fails at the base revision. (2) The budget snapshot is taken once per pick and reused for every candidate, asserted by a call-count test in the same file. (3) A test asserts the selections for the fixture are identical with and without the shared read. (4) reckon crew pick --replay 20 against live state records a median per-node latency at or under 3,000 ms and a maximum at or under 8,000 ms, logged with the base-revision median for the same 20 nodes, in a log under this run's directory and quoted in the manifest. (5) tests/test_picker.py and the existing routing tests that exercise the changed verdicts (find them with grep for _context_fit_verdict and _competence_verdict under tests/) pass with zero added failure ids against the base.\n (6) The replay summary in reckon/crew/picker/replay.py reports median Jev latency only over rows that made a Jev call, with the count of those rows beside it. It counts refused-model picks from each picked backend's serving observation at replay time, so the figure can be non-zero, and a test with a fixture probe returning refused for the picked model shows a count of 1. This node owns reckon/crew/picker/__init__.py, snapshot.py, replay.py and reckon/crew/routing.py, and does not edit prompts.py, the templates or lane_context.py, which a concurrent node owns.",
